  "beats": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "A vaccine can be ruined long before it reaches your arm. And no one would see it."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Most vaccines must stay between two and eight degrees Celsius."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Too warm, and they lose strength. Frozen, many are damaged for good."
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "So they travel a cold chain: an unbroken line of cold, from factory to clinic."
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Refrigerated trucks and insulated boxes keep them in range."
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Data loggers record the temperature around the clock."
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "text": "Some vials carry a label that darkens with too much heat."
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "text": "At the pharmacy, a dedicated fridge is checked every workday."
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "text": "If it slips out of range, doses are set aside until experts decide if they're usable."
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "text": "A vaccine only protects you if the cold held, all the way to your arm."
    }
  ],
